speechtext:
1.	What is the composition of the national capital commission?
2.	What are the duties and powers of the commission?
Answer: 1. The commission consists of 20 members appointed by the governor in council. Its membership comprises a member from each of the 10 provinces; at least two members from the city of Ottawa; at least one member from the city of Hull; and at least one member from a local municipality in Ontario and Quebec respectively, other than the cities of Ottawa and Hull.
Commissioners hold office during pleasure for not more than two consecutive terms, each term not to exceed four years.
2. The duties and powers of the commission are as follows:
(a)	Duties, to prepare plans for and assist in the development, conservation and improvement of the national capital region in order that the nature and character of the seat of the government of Canada may be in accordance with its national significance.
(b)	Powers,
(a)	acquire, hold, administer or develop property;
(b)	sell, grant, convey, lease or otherwise dispose of or make available to any person any property, subject to such conditions and limitations as it considers necessary or desirable;
(c)	construct, maintain and operate parks, squares, highways, parkways, bridges, buildings and any other works;
(d)	maintain and improve any property of the commission, or any other property under the control and management of a department, at the request of the authority or minister in charge thereof;
(e)	co-operate or engage in joint projects with, or make grants to, local municipalities or other authorities for the improvement, development or maintenance of property;
(f)	construct, maintain and operate, or grant concessions for the operation of, places of entertainment, amusement, recreation, refreshment, or other places of public interest

Questions
or accommodation upon any property of the commission;
(g)	administer, preserve and maintain any historic place or historic museum;
(h)	conduct investigations and researches in connection with the planning of the national capital region; and
(i)	generally, do and authorize such things as are incidental or conducive to the attainment of the objects and purposes of the commission and the exercise of its powers.

Mr. Speaker, I should like to point out that the hon. member for Quebec West (Mr. Plourde), the seconder of the motion, has not yet participated in the debate. Now, citation 165 specifies those who take part in the debate, where it is said;

But if the seconder should rise and say only a word or two, for instance, that he seconds the motion, he is precluded from again addressing the house.

But the hon. member for Quebec West, when he seconded the motion of the hon. member for Roberval (Mr. Gauthier), last Thursday, did not say a word. He did not even rise to say that he seconded the motion; he remained silent.

Order. I believe the hon. member for Quebec West (Mr. Plourde) had the floor at six o'clock. Before he resumes his remarks perhaps I should give to the house a message which should have been conveyed at six o'clock. That is, pursuant to provisional standing order 3 9A, the questions to be raised at 10.30 this evening are as follows:
The hon. member for Bow River (Mr. Woolliams) administration of justice; confining of juveniles in federal penitentiaries;

Second, the hon. member for Argenteuil-Deux-Montagnes (Mr. Drouin), Indian affairs, St. Gabriel, and the repaving of a road.
